{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB11264 - MONDO_2319",
  "verbalization": "Calcium Glycerophosphate is implicated in the pathogenesis of Phosphorus Metabolism Disease through a series of interconnected pathways that involve hypercalcemia. The evidence suggests that Calcium Glycerophosphate is associated with Hypercalcemia Disease, which in turn is linked to other phosphorus-related conditions. This relationship is supported by the indication of Calcium Glycerophosphate in Hypercalcemia Disease, which is a known contributor to elevated calcium levels in the blood. The mechanism by which Calcium Glycerophosphate influences hypercalcemia likely involves its role in modulating calcium homeostasis, potentially through interactions with calcium-binding proteins or by affecting the renal excretion of calcium. The presence of multiple pathways—each leading to Phosphorus Metabolism Disease—indicates a broader systemic impact, possibly involving the regulation of phosphate metabolism and its downstream effects on cellular processes. The consistent scores across the three paths suggest a robust and reliable association, though further mechanistic validation is needed to confirm the direct causal relationship.",
